- [ ] add basic rhythme and freq judging
  - when judging turned on, create a sound stream for real time input
  - only show the judging result on burst detected, which means don't judge the missed notes
  - find the nearest note when burst is heard, and show color which ranges from green to light
  - by default the final score is computed by the weighted rhythme difference and freq difference
  - user can adjust the weight that rhythme difference contributes to the result and freq difference
    contributes to the result in the settings
  - do not show the numbered score, but store the result for further usage

- [x] generate song automatically
  - [x] use Magenta.js (Google, runs in browser via TensorFlow.js) for ML-based melody generation
        models: MusicRNN (seed + continue), MusicVAE (free-form sampling), MelodyRNN (full melody from scratch)
        output is NoteSequence (MIDI pitch + start/end times) → convert to scale degrees relative to song key → emit .mmd
  - [x] generated songs plug into existing quiz engine
        note: model weights are ~20MB; MusicVAE is slow on CPU
  - [x] write scripted generator based on the idea of levy's flight

- [ ] build a pipeline of .gp to .mmd
  - use guitarpro (Python) to parse .gp/.gpx files into bars, notes (MIDI pitch + duration), BPM, track names
  - select melody track: prefer lead guitar or highest-pitched non-drum track; allow user override
  - detect key: use key signature from file if present, otherwise apply Krumhansl-Schmuckler
    algorithm on pitch class distribution, or let user specify manually
  - convert to intermediate JSON: { key, bpm, sections, measures, notes: [{midiPitch, speed}] }
  - convert MIDI pitch → jianpu scale degree (1-7) + octaveShift relative to detected key
  - convert note duration (ticks) → speed value (1=quarter, 2=eighth, etc.) with tie handling
    for notes that don't fit standard grid values
  - emit .mmd: section markers from GP section markers, inline % bpm: X % for tempo changes
  - data source: Guitar Pro files from MuseScore.com (export as .gpx) or direct .gp archives

